Drug Administration  FDA   The FDA has determined that such clearance or approval is not necessary  this laboratory has established and verified the test s accuracy and precision  Added by  Grace T  McKee MD Signed On  12 REMOVED_MONTH 04 at 16 32 GROSS DESCRIPTION  Received fresh   labeled REMOVE_PATIENT_NAME  patient s name  unit number and  left breast  is a 20 x 20 x 4 5cm modified radical mastectomy specimen  with an attached skin ellipse measuring REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 0 3cm  an axillary tail measuring 11 x REMOVED_DATE x 1 5cm  and a strip of muscle measuring REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 0 2cm  The nipple is inverted with areas of nodularity around it  and there are two pink red bruises measuring REMOVED_DATE x 1 0cm in greatest dimension over the skin  The specimen is inked and serially sectioned into twelve slices from medial to lateral  and reveals a firm stellate mass  identified by a clip  measuring REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 1 0cm  The mass is found towards the middle of the specimen in slices seven and eight  The closest deep margin is 3 5cm from the mass  and 2 5cm from the skin  One arm of the stellate mass extends inferiorly and towards the axillary tail forming a firm nodularity but no discrete mass  and measures REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 2 0cm and is found in slices eight and nine  This nodularity is 4 0cm from the black ink  There is a second  ill defined  stellate  firm area measuring REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 2 5cm  which abuts the nipple and is found in slices five and six  This area is 3 0cm medial to the stellate mass identified by the clip and is 4 0cm from the black ink  The rest of the breast tissue consists of grossly unremarkable fibrofatty tissue  The specimen is submitted as follows  A  Portion of stellate mass and closest black inked margin  B D  Further representative sections of the stellate mass  E  Nipple with mass abutting nipple  F and G  Further representative sections of lesion beneath nipple  H I  Representative sections of nodularity extending inferiorly and medially from scar  Multiple lymph nodes are identified in the axillary tail and submitted individually in cassettes J P  A large  possibly matted lymph node bisected and submitted in cassette Q and R  One lymph node bisected and now in S T  U  Six small lymph nodes submitted entirely  Most of the lymph nodes contain necrotic pasty material  V W  Grossly unremarkable breast tissue  NOTE  The tumor extensively involves lymphatic vessels  Definite stromal invasion is not seen  but micro invasion cannot be completely excluded  The margin of the specimen appears free of tumor  Resident  SHABIN NANJI  MD                                                                                 SOURCE CARE UNIT  MGH INPATIENT  FLR UNKNOWN  REPORTS TO  Kevin S  Hughes  M D  PART   BREAST LEFT  MODIFIED RADICAL MASTECTOMY , Negative.
Accession Number   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Report Status  Final Type  Surgical Pathology Pathology Report   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  OPERATION DATE  25 REMOVED_MONTH 93 ACCESSIONED ON  25 REMOVED_MONTH 93 at 11 45 CLINICAL DATA  Bilateral breast lesions                                                                                      FINAL DIAGNOSIS      SUPERIOR MEDIAL  LEFT BREAST   BIOPSY  1  LOBULAR CARCINOMA IN SITU AND ATYPICAL LOBULAR HYPERPLASIA  SEE NOTE  2  FIBROCYSTIC CHANGES WITH MICROCYSTS  FIBROSIS  APOCRINE METAPLASIA  SCLEROSING ADENOSIS AND MICROCALCIFICATIONS  NOTE  Carcinoma in situ involves inked surgical margin   LOWER LESION OF LEFT BREAST   BIOPSY  1  ATYPICAL LOBULAR HYPERPLASIA  2  FIBROCYSTIC CHANGES WITH MICROCYSTS  FIBROSIS  APOCRINE METAPLASIA  SCLEROSING ADENOSIS AND MICROCALCIFICATIONS  BREAST  RIGHT   BIOPSY  1  INFILTRATING DUCTAL CARCINOMA  60    POORLY DIFFERENTIATED GRADE REMOVED_DATE  AND INTRADUCTAL CARCINOMA  40    COMEDO TYPE  SEE NOTE  2  LOBULAR HYPERPLASIA  NOTE  The infiltrating carcinoma extends to the inked margin in multiple places  Definitive vascular invasion is not identified  The invasive carcinoma measures 2 5cm in greatest dimensions  Frozen section diagnosis is confirmed  Diagnosis by  Christophe Girardet  M D  Signed On  30 REMOVED_MONTH 93 at 16 15                                                                                     S PHASE ANALYSIS Addendum     BREAST   SUPERIOR MEDIAL LEFT  BIOPSY   BRDU UPTAKE  PERCENTAGE OF CELLS IN S PHASE   6   SEE NOTE  NOTE  The breast tumor tissue  after being incubated in medium containing a thymidine analog  bromodeoxyuridine  was processed and stained by immunohistochemical methods to detect those cells which were replicating DNA  Positive cells  cells in S phase  were counted by microscopic visualization of the breast tissue  In this case  the predominant lesion present in the tumor submitted for BRDU uptake was in situ carcinoma  Results from 372 MGH cases of invasive ductal carcinoma have shown cells in S phase ranging from 0  to 40   with a mean 6 3  and median of 5  Benign lesions  and invasive lobular carcinomas have consistantly shown very low counts  less than 3    Added by  Ann D  Thor  M D  Signed On  02 REMOVED_MONTH 93 at 16 28     HORMONE BINDING ASSAY Addendum     RESULTS OF HORMONE BINDING ASSAYS AS REPORTED BY DAVID T  MACLAUGHLIN  PhD   726  REMOVED_CASE_ID   estrogen receptor  Negative  progesterone receptor  Negative  Added by  Frederick Carl Koerner  M D  Signed On  13 REMOVED_MONTH 93 at 10 43     RECEPTOR IMMUNOHISTOCHEMISTRY Addendum     RESULTS OF STAINING OF FROZEN TISSUE FOR HORMONE RECEPTOR PROTEINS BREAST  RIGHT   The tumor cells do not stain for estrogen receptor protein  The tumor cells do not stain for progesterone receptor protein  See note  Note  In this laboratory  the results of prospective staining for estrogen receptor protein of 100 unfixed breast tumors were compared with determinations of estrogen receptors by biochemical assay  93  of tumors that reacted with this antibody were positive for estrogen receptors by biochemical assay  100  of tumors that failed to stain were negative for estrogen receptors by biochemical assay  In this laboratory  we also compared the results of staining for progesterone receptor protein of 50 breast carcinomas with determinations of progesterone receptors by a hormone binding assay  Seventy three percent of tumors that stained with this antibody contained progesterone receptors by the biochemical assay  and 91  of tumors that failed to stain lacked the receptors  THE RESULTS OF IMMUNOHISTOCHEMICAL STAINING FOR PROGESTERONE RECEPTOR PROTEIN ARE FOR INVESTIGATIONAL USE ONLY  THEY MUST NOT BE USED FOR CLINICAL PURPOSES  Added by  Frederick Carl Koerner  M D  Signed On  27 REMOVED_MONTH 93 at 10 12 GROSS DESCRIPTION  Received in two parts  each  labeled REMOVE_PATIENT_NAME  patient s name and unit number  Labeled  Superior medial lesion  left breast  is a REMOVED_